### What is UUIDv7 and why is it popular?

UUIDv7 embeds a Unix timestamp in the most significant 48 bits, followed by random bits. This makes the ids roughly time-ordered, which gives B-tree index locality and avoids the random-insert fragmentation of UUIDv4.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.util.UUID;

public class UUIDv7Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Java 17+ does not have native UUIDv7; use a library
        // Example using com.fasterxml.uuid:java-uuid-generator
        // UUID id = Generators.timeBasedEpochGenerator().generate();

        // The first 48 bits are the Unix timestamp in milliseconds
        // Remaining bits are random
        // Result: 019234ab-cdef-7xxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx
    }
}
```

### Why is UUIDv7 not a distributed sequence?

The time prefix comes from the generating node's wall clock. Within one node, ids are reliably increasing. But across nodes, clocks drift.

```text
Node A clock: 10:00:00.000 ──▶ generates UUID_A
Node B clock: 10:00:00.003 ──▶ generates UUID_B (3ms later by B's clock)

But what if Node A's clock is 5ms ahead of true time?
Node A actual:  09:59:59.995 ──▶ UUID_A timestamp says 10:00:00.000
Node B actual:  10:00:00.003 ──▶ UUID_B timestamp says 10:00:00.003

UUID_A < UUID_B by timestamp, but UUID_B was actually created AFTER UUID_A
Clock skew: 5ms difference makes the ordering unreliable across nodes
```

For true ordering, use an explicit authoritative timestamp column, a real sequence, or logical clocks.

### Why must UUIDv7 be stored compactly?

A UUID is 128 bits (16 bytes). Stored as `CHAR(36)`, it becomes 36 bytes plus overhead: more than double the size.

```sql
-- Wrong: CHAR(36) wastes space and bloats indexes
CREATE TABLE transactions (
    id CHAR(36) PRIMARY KEY,  -- 36+ bytes per row and per index entry
    amount NUMERIC(18,2)
);

-- Correct in PostgreSQL: native uuid type (16 bytes)
CREATE TABLE transactions (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY,  -- 16 bytes
    amount NUMERIC(18,2)
);

-- Correct in MySQL: BINARY(16)
CREATE TABLE transactions (
    id BINARY(16) PRIMARY KEY,  -- 16 bytes
    amount DECIMAL(18,2)
);
```

Since the primary key is embedded in every secondary index in InnoDB, a CHAR(36) key bloats the entire table's index footprint and wastes buffer-pool memory.

### How much storage difference does it make?

Rajesh measured at National Bank of India with 10 million transactions.

```text
┌──────────────┬──────────────┬───────────────┐
│ Storage Type │ PK Size      │ Index Overhead│
├──────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────┤
│ BINARY(16)   │ 160 MB       │ +16 bytes/row │
│ CHAR(36)     │ 360 MB       │ +36 bytes/row │
│ Difference   │ +125%        │ +125% per idx │
└──────────────┴──────────────┴───────────────┘
```

Convert to text only at the application edge for display.

### Why does UUIDv7 leak creation time?

The first 48 bits are a plainly decodable Unix timestamp in milliseconds.

```java
package com.bank.util;

import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.UUID;

public class UUIDv7TimeExtractor {

    public static Instant extractTimestamp(UUID uuid) {
        long msb = uuid.getMostSignificantBits();
        long timestampMs = msb >>> 16; // top 48 bits
        return Instant.ofEpochMilli(timestampMs);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Anyone with the UUID can extract the creation time
        // UUID: 019234ab-cdef-7xxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx
        // Timestamp: 2026-06-15T10:30:00Z
    }
}
```

This is harmless for an internal identifier but means UUIDv7 must never serve as a secret token.

### Why is UUIDv7 not a secret or auth token?

Because part of the value is predictable time and only the tail is random, a UUIDv7 is not unguessable in the cryptographic sense.

```java
package com.bank.security;

import java.security.SecureRandom;
import java.util.Base64;

public class TokenService {

    // Wrong: UUIDv7 as a password-reset token
    // The timestamp is predictable, reducing entropy

    // Correct: fully random, cryptographically secure token
    public String generateSecureToken() {
        byte[] bytes = new byte[32]; // 256 bits
        new SecureRandom().nextBytes(bytes);
        return Base64.getUrlEncoder().withoutPadding().encodeToString(bytes);
    }
}
```

### When should you use UUIDv7 versus alternatives?

| Use Case | Recommended ID Type |
|---|---|
| Distributed primary key | UUIDv7 (locality + uniqueness) |
| Strict global ordering | Database sequence or logical clock |
| Session or reset token | 256-bit SecureRandom |
| Public API identifier | UUIDv7 (if time leak is acceptable) |
| Legal invoice number | Gapless database sequence |

### How do you use UUIDv7 with JPA/Hibernate?

Rajesh maps the UUID as the entity id at National Bank of India.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.UUID;

@Entity
@Table(name = "transactions")
public class Transaction {

    @Id
    @Column(columnDefinition = "uuid")
    private UUID id;

    @Column(precision = 18, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal amount;

